# Qwen3.8-27B (August 2026), NVIDIA's NVFP4 build. Dense 27B, 22 GB,
# Apache-2.0. It thinks by default (qwen3 reasoning parser, reasoning_effort
# xhigh | medium | low) and calls tools (qwen3_coder parser). 61.7 SWE-bench
# Pro and 73.0 Terminal-Bench on Qwen's card.
#
# 256k natively. VLLM_LONG stretches it with the YaRN setting from the model
# card, and that has been checked here with scripts/context-test.py: with a
# 950k window it called the tool and returned all three codes at 97k, 245k and
# 507k tokens. Qwen3-Coder-Next lost tool calling under the same stretch, so
# the check matters.
#
# The cost is speed. A dense model reads every weight for every token: about
# 12 tok/s on a short prompt, and prefill falls from 900 tok/s to under 300 as
# the context grows, so a cold 500k prompt takes 22 minutes.
HF_REPO=nvidia/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4
SERVED_NAME=qwen3.8-27b
MAX_LEN=262144
LONG_MAX_LEN=900000
ROPE_ORIG=262144
ROPE_STYLE=qwen35
TOOL_PARSER=qwen3_coder
REASONING_PARSER=qwen3
EFFORT_ALIAS=1
# 16-bit cache, which is what the results above were measured with. 16 of 64
# layers are full attention with 4 KV heads of 256, and the linear-attention
# layers keep state alongside: about 66 KB per token. 0.75 of 121.7 GiB holds
# 950k tokens of it, and a single request cannot use quite all of that: a 935k
# prompt was read to the end, ran out of blocks and started over. Hence 900k.
# At 0.80 the GPU driver ran out of memory during warm-up and the server hung.
#
# NVIDIA's recipe for this checkpoint uses an FP8 cache, which halves the cost
# and makes room for the card's full 1.01M: KV_DTYPE=fp8_e4m3, MEM_UTIL=0.68,
# MIN_FREE_GB=84, LONG_MAX_LEN=1010000. Recall at that precision has not been
# measured here.
KV_DTYPE=auto
MEM_UTIL=0.75
MIN_FREE_GB=92
EXTRA_ARGS="--max-num-batched-tokens 32768"
